= Working memory =

== Overview ==
'''Working memory''' refers to limited-capacity systems and processes that keep information available for ongoing thinking while allowing it to be manipulated, updated, or integrated with other information.

Working memory is used in reasoning, language comprehension, mental arithmetic, planning, and many forms of learning.

== Working memory and short-term memory ==
Short-term memory usually emphasizes temporary retention.

Working memory emphasizes temporary retention together with active use or control.

Some theories treat working memory as a broader system that includes short-term storage; others model the relationship differently.

Therefore:

<pre>
working memory ≠ short-term memory by definition
</pre>

== Multicomponent model ==
Baddeley's influential framework includes a phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, central executive, and episodic buffer.

These components explain useful dissociations, but the model is not the definition of working memory itself.

== Competing models ==
Other approaches emphasize activated portions of long-term memory, attentional focus, interference, temporal distinctiveness, executive attention, or neural population dynamics.

== Capacity ==
Working memory is limited.

There is no universal fixed number of independent slots that applies to every task. Capacity depends on representation, chunking, knowledge, modality, interference, attention, and measurement.

== Cognitive load ==
[[Cognitive load]] research builds on the fact that working memory is constrained during learning and problem solving.

Instructional design can reduce avoidable processing demands without increasing underlying working-memory capacity.

== Related concepts ==
[[Memory]], [[Attention]], and [[Cognitive load]] provide the immediate conceptual context for temporary maintenance, selection, and processing limits.

